Notes on the wiring decisions:

- **UnitOfWork** registers all four SObject types in a single `fflib_Application.UnitOfWorkFactory`, so one `commitWork()` can insert Accounts, Contacts on those accounts, Opportunities on those accounts, and OpportunityContactRoles bridging them, all registered via `registerNew` with relationship fields set on the records themselves.
- **Selector** maps by `SObjectType`, which is how `Application.Selector.newInstance(Account.SObjectType)` resolves to `AccountsSelector` (as used in `AccountsServiceImpl`).
- **Domain** is constructed with the `DomainFactory(SelectorFactory, Map<SObjectType, Type>)` overload — this makes `Application.Domain.newInstance(Set<Id>)` fetch the records through the `Selector` factory (via `AccountsSelector.selectById`) before wrapping them with the `Accounts.Constructor`, exactly as required. (Since `Accounts` lacks a public no-arg `construct()` variant, the `Accounts.Constructor` class provided in the org is the correct registration type.)
- All four factories are exposed as `public static` with `private set`, so unit tests can call `Application.UnitOfWork.setMock(...)` etc. while production wiring stays immutable; the lazy getters keep production initialization intact even after tests set mocks.
- The class is `inherited sharing` (default), matching the rest of the layer so sharing enforcement follows each caller (the service itself is `with sharing` as designed).

Here are the two deliverables.

**Design notes (brief):**
- `Opportunities` handles the insert case in `onAfterInsert` (Ids only exist after commit) and the update case in `onAfterUpdate` (comparing against `Trigger.oldMap` via the `existingRecords` map). Since fflib invokes each hook exactly once per trigger invocation with the full record list, the service call is inherently bulkified: ids are gathered into one `Set<Id>` and `startFulfilment` is invoked at most once per invocation, and only when at least one opportunity qualifies.
- The "already Closed Won, edited again" case is filtered out by comparing `StageName` in the old map — only a transition *to* 'Closed Won' from another stage qualifies.
- The service is obtained via `Application.Service.newInstance(...)`, so tests can substitute it with `Application.Service.setMock`.
- The trigger handles all seven events purely via `fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class)`, which resolves the standard inner `Constructor` class.

