```apex
public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    /**
     * Normalizes the Phone field of the given contacts so it contains only
     * digits and an optional leading '+' (e.g. '+44 (20) 7946-0018' becomes
     * '+442079460018').
     *
     * All contacts are saved with a single partial-success DML, so one record
     * failing validation does not block the others.
     *
     * @param contactIds  Ids of the contacts to process (up to 200 per call).
     * @return            Map from each contact Id that could NOT be saved to
     *                    its first error message; empty map when all saved.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> errorsByContactId = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return errorsByContactId;
        }

        // One bulk SOQL for all Ids; only contacts that actually have a phone.
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
              AND Phone != null
        ];

        // Strip everything that is not a digit or '+'.
        for (Contact con : contacts) {
            con.Phone = con.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
        }

        // Single partial-success update: a failing record doesn't roll back the rest.
        List<Database.SaveResult> saveResults = Database.update(contacts, false);

        // Collect the first error of every failed record, keyed by its Id.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < saveResults.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult result = saveResults[i];
            if (!result.isSuccess()) {
                errorsByContactId.put(
                    contacts[i].Id,
                    result.getErrors()[0].getMessage()
                );
            }
        }

        return errorsByContactId;
    }
}
```

Notes on your approach — it was sound, so I changed as little as possible:

- **One SOQL instead of a loop of queries.** You said "loop over those contacts"; I query them all with a single `WHERE Id IN :contactIds` and filter `Phone != null` there. That keeps the method bulk-safe (even though 200 records is modest) and puts the "has a phone" condition in the query rather than an extra branch. The `if (con.Phone != null)` guard is unnecessary because the query already guarantees it.
- **`with sharing`** added as the safe default for a class touching records; if this runs from an automated context where FLS sharing isn't desired you can drop it, but it's the right starting point.
- **One deviation worth knowing about:** contacts whose phone is already clean still get included in the `Database.update`, exactly as you specified. If you ever want to avoid touching unchanged records (fewer rows in setup-audit, no firing of triggers/flows for no-op saves), filter with `if (con.Phone != con.Phone.replaceAll(...))` before adding to the update list — I left that out to match your stated behavior.
- Edge case: a `Phone` consisting entirely of punctuation normalizes to `''` (empty string), which would still be submitted. If your dialer treats that as invalid, add `AND Phone != ''` to the query or skip empties in the loop.

