```apex
public with sharing class AccountRatingService {

    private static final String RATING_HOT = 'Hot';
    private static final String RATING_WARM = 'Warm';
    private static final String RATING_COLD = 'Cold';
    private static final Decimal HOT_THRESHOLD = 100000;

    /**
     * Recalculates the Rating of the given accounts from their open pipeline.
     *
     * Runs asynchronously (@future) so opportunity saves are not slowed down,
     * and takes a Set of account ids so that a batch of up to 200 opportunity
     * DML rows costs exactly ONE future call, not one per opportunity.
     *
     * Rules:
     *   Hot  - at least one open opportunity with Amount >= 100,000
     *   Warm - open opportunities, but none with Amount >= 100,000
     *   Cold - no open opportunities
     */
    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One aggregate query gives us the largest open deal per account.
        // Accounts with no open opportunities simply don't appear in the
        // result, which maps cleanly to Cold. MAX() ignores null Amounts,
        // so an open opp with a blank Amount still counts as Warm.
        Map<Id, AggregateResult> maxByAccount = new Map<Id, AggregateResult>([
            SELECT AccountId, MAX(Amount) maxAmount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
                AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]);

        // Read current ratings so we only write accounts whose value changed.
        // This also silently skips accounts deleted between the opportunity
        // DML and this future execution.
        Map<Id, Account> accounts = new Map<Id, Account>([
            SELECT Id, Rating
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ]);

        List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for (Id accountId : accounts.keySet()) {
            AggregateResult agg = maxByAccount.get(accountId);
            Decimal maxAmount = agg == null
                ? null
                : (Decimal) agg.get('maxAmount');

            String newRating;
            if (agg == null) {
                newRating = RATING_COLD;
            } else if (maxAmount != null && maxAmount >= HOT_THRESHOLD) {
                newRating = RATING_HOT;
            } else {
                newRating = RATING_WARM;
            }

            Account acc = accounts.get(accountId);
            if (acc.Rating != newRating) {
                toUpdate.add(new Account(Id = accountId, Rating = newRating));
            }
        }

        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

## What I did differently, and why

**1. One future call per transaction, not per opportunity (the important one).**
You asked for `AccountRatingService.recalculate(opp.AccountId)` per opportunity. With CPQ inserting/updating batches of 200, that would be up to 200 future calls in a single transaction. The synchronous limit is **50 `@future` calls per transaction**, so call #51 would throw a `System.LimitException` and roll back the whole CPQ batch. Instead, the trigger collects distinct `AccountId`s and makes a **single** future call with the set. The public signature of `recalculate` still exists — it just takes a `Set<Id>` — and it's bulkified internally too (one aggregate query, one `update`). This keeps your saves fast (still async, still one call) and actually makes it faster: 1 future call + 2 queries per batch instead of 200 calls + 200×2 queries.

**2. Also recalculates the *old* account when `AccountId` changes.**
Your spec says recalc on insert or on `StageName`/`Amount` change. If an opp is re-parented from Account A to Account B and nothing else changes, B updates but A keeps a stale `Rating`. I added the old account to the recalc set. It's one extra line and prevents silent data drift; remove it if you truly want only the literal spec.

**3. Only writes accounts whose `Rating` actually changed.**
In a 200-row batch, many opportunities map to the same accounts, and most recalcs end with the same value. Re-writing an unchanged `Rating` burns DML rows, fires Account triggers/flows, and bumps `LastModifiedDate`. Skipping no-op writes avoids all of that. The pre-query for current ratings is one cheap query and also guards against `INVALID_CROSS_REFERENCE_KEY` if an account was deleted before the future method ran.

**4. `IsClosed = false` rather than a stage-name list.**
"Open opportunity" is a `IsClosed` checkbox, which respects your org's stage configuration (including any custom closed stages). Hardcoding stage names like `Closed Won` would break the moment CPQ adds a stage.

**5. Trigger guards.**
`after insert`/`after update` only (ratings are derived from stored values, so `before` is pointless), skips opportunities with a blank `AccountId`, and no-ops on an empty set — a trigger should never enqueue work it doesn't need.

One thing to be aware of: `@future` is fire-and-forget, so a rating is eventually consistent (typically within seconds). If you ever need guarantees or chaining, the same service body drops into `Queueable` with almost no changes.

## Solution

The one thing I changed versus the literal instruction: I do **not** call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` per line. That method runs a `SELECT` on `Product2` on every call, so a 200-line CPQ quote would burn up to 200 queries against the 100-SOQL governor limit and throw on insert. Instead, I keep all discount logic inside `PricingRules` (nothing copied) by querying the products' families once for the whole batch and resolving each distinct family through `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(...)`. Same single source of truth, bulk-safe.

Other details:

- **Before insert** means we mutate `Trigger.new` in place — no DML, no recursion, and the rep's own value is already on the record.
- Only lines with `Discount == null` are touched; a typed `0` is a real value and is preserved, because `null` and `0` are distinguishable before insert.
- `standardDiscountForFamily` handles unknown/`null` families by returning 0, so a product with no family simply gets the 0 standard discount.
- `inherited sharing` on the handler so it never widens the calling context's sharing; the trigger itself runs in system context as triggers do.

