Design notes (not part of the returned files):

- **Correctness across all contexts.** Insert, delete and undelete recount every affected account from the source of truth (an aggregate `COUNT` on open cases), so created-open, created-closed, deleted and undeleted cases are all covered, and an account whose last open case goes away is explicitly written `0` (accounts with no aggregate row default to `0`, never blank). Update only recounts when `AccountId` or `IsClosed` actually changed, and adds both the old and new account so case moves keep both counts accurate.
- **Limits.** Per trigger execution: at most one aggregate SOQL on Case, one SOQL on Account, and one `update` DML — well within the 200-case budget. Accounts whose stored value already matches are filtered out before DML, so no-op updates are skipped and no recursion loop can occur (and Account has no trigger in this org).
- **Bulk safety.** All Ids are gathered into a `Set` first, queries use bind-in collections, and the DML is a single list update. No static recursion guard is used deliberately: each genuine Case transaction must recount, and TAF already provides per-record recursion tracking if ever needed.
- **Cases without an account** are ignored (`null` `AccountId` is filtered out before querying).
- The action is registered per context via the four `Trigger_Action__mdt` records pointing at the existing `sObject_Trigger_Setting__mdt` record `Case`, each with `Order__c` 10 and the required `Description__c`.

